    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/international-students": {
      "title": "International Students",
      "description": "International Students : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake International Students Careers International Students Welcome to the University of Massachusetts Career and Professional Development Hub. We are delighted to have you here and look forward to working with you as you prepare to embark on your professional career. As an international student, you have a multi-faceted background that will be an asset in your career. The Career Hub has curated a handful of resources to aid you in putting your best foot forward during your job search. Office of Global Affairs The Office of Global Affairs (or OGA) manages many facets of UMass Amherst's interactions with other countries, including helping international students go through the proper procedures for working while attending UMass Amherst and as a recent graduate. OGA's webpage has the most up to date information on working in the United States as an international student at UMass. If you are planning on working in the United States, it is recommended you attend one of the OPT/CPT workshops that OGA hosts each semester. Curriculum Practical Training (CPT) - Working while at UMass Curricular Practical Training (CPT) is defined as \"alternate work/study, internship, cooperative education, or any other type of required internship or practicum which is offered by sponsoring employers through cooperative agreements with the school.\" CPT is a benefit of F-1 status. You are not guaranteed CPT and must meet all eligibility requirements to be authorized. Please be aware that CPT opportunities vary within UMass. Some programs may allow CPT while school is in session, while others only permit CPT during summer and/or winter terms. Students should determine this by contacting the appropriate person in their college. For more information on CPT, visit the CPT Information Page on the IPO site or attend an information session while classes are in session. Optional Practical Training (OPT) - Working after UMass OPT is a benefit of F-1 student immigration status and provides for an initial 12-months of off-campus work authorization anywhere in the United States for international students who have been enrolled full-time for a minimum of one academic year (considered to be 9-months or two full semesters). There are three different types of OPT: Pre-completion OPT (employment before completion of studies) Post-completion OPT (employment after completion of studies and graduation STEM OPT extension (extension of previous Post-completion OPT for those students who graduated in a STEM field ( List of Fields Labeled STEM ) You may file your Post-Completion OPT up to 90-days prior to your completion of studies date, and up to 60-days after graduation/program end date. However, waiting to apply until 60-days after your graduation/program end date is strongly discouraged, as it may jeopardize your ability to utilize the full 12-months of OPT eligibility because of processing delays. For information on how to apply for OPT, visit the OPT Application page on the OGA site or attend an information session while classes are in session. Searching for a Job Utilize Job Boards with an International Candidate Focus A job board is a website that posts open positions. While there are many websites that does this, not all of them have filters that narrow down opportunities that are open to international applicants. Handshake is UMass Amherst's career platform, and has many functions to help students find an opportunity that is right for them. If you are unfamiliar with how to use Handshake to search for jobs, begin with our resource guide: How to Use Handshake . UMass Amherst Collections: Companies that Hire International Students UMass Amherst Collections are curated lists of employers specifically tagged for UMass Amherst students by UMass Amherst Career Services. The “Hires International Students” collection is a list of employers who have hired UMass Amherst international students into full-time jobs since 2020. To view the list of employers in the Hires International Students Collection : Log into Handshake Click Career Center on the left navigation menu. Scroll down to the section for University of Massachusetts Amherst collections and click on \"Hires International Students\" to view the list of employers. Follow the companies you are most interested in to receive a Handshake notification when they add new postings. Search Filters: Find Companies that Hire International Students When on the Job Search page of Handshake, t here are two job search filters in Handshake that are specifically helpful for international students. Applying the UMass Amherst collections option \"Hires International Students\" will produce a curated list of active Handshake postings from employers who have hired past UMass Amherst international students. This filter can be used in combination with any of the other job search filters to further refine your search. To view jobs posted by employers that hire international students: Log into Handshake Click Jobs on the left navigation menu Next to the search bar, click \"UMass Amherst collections\" and select \"Hires International Students\" Within the “All Filters” section, there are filter options for work authorization. These are based upon the criteria the employer selected when entering the job posting in Handshake. You may select one or more of these options to apply to your search. Open to US visa sponsorship Open to Optional Practical Training (OPT) Open to Curricular Practical Training (CPT) No US work authorization required Unknown Resources Beyond Handshake OPT Nation's List of Companies that Hire International Students : Many companies have an established history of hiring both domestic and international candidates. By searching these companies' job postings, you will increase the likelihood of finding positions that offer the potential for sponsorship. Applying for Jobs: Resumes and Curriculum Vitae (CVs) When applying for jobs in the United States, you may be asked for either a resume or a curriculum vitae. While both documents summarize your professional experience, there are differences between the two. Resume A one-to-two page summary of your experience as it relates to the position for which you are applying. While a resume often includes professional experience related to your field, it will often include academic, leadership, research, or volunteer experiences as well so long as those experiences relate to the role. A resume is the most often used document when applying for positions in the United States. Curriculum Vitae (CV) A multi-page collection of all of your experience related to your course of study. Unlike a resume, a CV typically is not customized for every position. Rather, it tends to be a stable document that is added to as you gather experiences and accomplishments in your course of study. A CV is most often used when applying for positions in academia or research in the United States. Note that when applying for jobs outside of the United States, the term \"curriculum vitae\" is often used interchangeably with \"resume\". If you aren't sure whether you should be submitting a resume or CV, speak with a career counselor or faculty advisor. Additionally, if you are looking to apply for positions outside of the United States, it is helpful to see what the expectations for applications are in other countries . More about Resumes More about CVs International Student Resources These resources are dedicated to providing essential resources for navigating your career path as an international student. From internships and co-op opportunities to visa regulations and work authorization guidelines, these resources are tailored to address the specific needs and challenges faced by international students studying in the US. International Student and Scholar Services (UMass Amherst) International Student and Scholar Services (UMass Amherst) Employers that Hired UMass International Students (Handshake access required) Employers that Hired UMass International Students (Handshake access required) H1B Visa Jobs H1B Visa Jobs The International Advantage The International Advantage International Student Job Search International Student Job Search MyVisaJobs MyVisaJobs OPT Nation OPT Nation United OPT United OPT UNIWORLD: Directories of American Firms Operating in Foreign Countries and Fore… UNIWORLD: Directories of American Firms Operating in Foreign Countries and Fore… Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
    },
    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/prospective-students": {
      "title": "Welcome Prospective Students and Parents!",
      "description": "Prospective Students : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Welcome Prospective Students and Parents! Welcome to the University of Massachusetts Amherst's Career Development and Professional Connections Hub. We are thrilled to have you and your family here, exploring the many opportunities and resources that the University has to offer. As a prospective student, you are probably curious about how your education at UMass will help prepare you for your chosen career. UMass uses a hub model approach to career advising, meaning there are specialized centers across campus to serve different students' specific professional needs. The University also hosts over 20 career fairs each year, as well as hundreds of career events including networking opportunities, professional skills training, and employer visits. Whether you need career resources for the plan you already have, or help finding a plan that works for you, UMass Career Development is here for you as you embark on your career journey. Resources for Prospective Students: With so many resources at your disposal, choosing where to start can be a little overwhelming. Here are a few to get you started: First-Year Career Pathways Get a head start in your academic and professional pursuits with the First-Year Career Pathways program. Greater Boston's vibrant and diverse industries—technology, finance, healthcare, biotech, and more—create the perfect environment to hone your career aspirations. Start your UMass Amherst journey and gain experience in one of the nation's most dynamic cities. Participating students will have the opportunity to join a select cohort of students on the Greater Boston campus in Newton, benefiting from a close-knit community while remaining connected to the vibrant city of Boston. Enjoy a dual-campus experience with a free shuttle connecting Newton and Amherst. Learn More about First-Year Career Pathways Learn More about First-Year Career Pathways Start Your Career Journey Your Career Journey, like other types of journeys, is the passage from one place or stage of life to another, with each stage bringing increased knowledge, learned skills, and new insights. The journey is made easier through assessments of self, majors, career fields, and employers, as well as skills development and professional connections.  UMass Career Services is here to guide you on this journey and to provide resources that will make the twists and turns easier and more effective. Learn more about what you can expect on the adventure that is being a UMass Amherst student! Career Journey > Overview Career Journey Overview Explore with Self-Assessments Much of your college experience will be reflecting on what you have already accomplished, and choosing experiences to lead you to your next wins. A great way to figure out what might work best for you next is to look inward. Self-assessments help you identify your unique strengths, skills, interests, and personality so that you can make informed career decisions. UMass Career Services utilizes a range of self-assessment tools to guide you throughout your academic career. Career Journey > Self Discovery and Career Exploration (ARCHIVE 02) UMass Self-Assessment Tools Career Outcomes of UMass Graduates While UMass has the most direct impact on students’ experiences and achievements while they are attending the University, we are also interested in our graduates’ achievements later in life and how they view the contributions their UMass Amherst experiences made to their professional, personal, and civic lives. In keeping with that, we track our students' successes every year through the First Destination Survey. Browse through the last few years of outcomes data and see where our students go and what they do. About > Student Outcomes UMass Undergraduate Outcome Data Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
    },
