    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/employers/why-recruit-umass": {
      "title": "Why Recruit",
      "description": "Why Recruit at UMass : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Why Recruit at UMass Careers Employers Why Recruit Umass Close Menu Employers: Quick Links Handshake Login Career Fairs UMass Amherst Academic Calendar Contact Us The University of Massachusetts Amherst—the flagship campus of the UMass system—is a nationally ranked public research university located in the scenic Pioneer Valley of Western Massachusetts, only 90 miles from Boston and 175 miles from New York City. UMass Amherst by the Numbers #1 Public Research University in New England #29 Top Public Universities (U.S. News & World Report 2026) 114 Undergraduate Degree Programs 135 Graduate Degree Programs (Master's and Doctoral) The UMass Amherst students are a diverse, inclusive community of dreamers, innovators, creators, and doers who are looking to make their mark on the world. They fearlessly challenge the conventions of today in pursuit of a better tomorrow. Today’s graduates are innovative, dynamic problem-solvers prepared to be citizens in a rapidly evolving global environment. UMass Enrollment Figures 23,000 Undergraduate Students (Fall 2025) 42% ALANA Undergraduate Students (Fall 2025) 72% In-State Students (Fall 2025) 8% International Undergraduate Students (Fall 2025) Outcome Highlights for the Class of 2025 The Class of 2025 has demonstrated unprecedented levels of success and engagement just within the first 6 months after their graduation. The data below represents the results from the First Destination Survey for the Class of 2025. 88% Undergraduate students who obtained a career outcome related to their field of study 40 Number of different states the Class of 2025 are working in post graduation 24 Number of different countries the Class of 2025 are working in post graduation Interactive Student Outcomes The following is an interactive content module for UMass Amherst's undergraduate student outcomes. An accessible version of this module can be found on the Student Outcome Text page. Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
    },
    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/employers/internship-program-best-practices": {
      "title": "Internship Program Best Practices",
      "description": "Internship Program Best Practices : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Internship Program Best Practices Careers Employers Internship Program Best Practices Close Menu Employers: Quick Links Handshake Login Career Fairs UMass Amherst Academic Calendar Contact Us Why start an internship program with UMass Amherst? Bringing on UMass Amherst students as interns can benefit any organization: Top students with knowledge and skills that can support projects you are struggling to complete Insights from Gen Z can provide new ideas and perspectives The interns serve as a brand ambassadors on campus to promote your company, especially if they have a great internship experience You will have UMass Career Services to help market your positions Compliance Message to Employers regarding Unpaid Internships The University of Massachusetts Amherst complies with the U.S. Department of Labor’s Wage and Hour Division (WHD) and the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) regarding unpaid internships. In order to protect themselves from any potential liability which could arise from the offering of an unpaid internship, the employer should make sure that the internship opportunity meets the guidelines developed by the Fair Labor Standards Act referenced above. Internship Program Resources Defining the Different Types of Field Experience Field experiences, also known as experiential learning opportunities, are structured learning opportunities that a student can engage in outside of the classroom and are intended to help students develop skills related to their major, as well as competencies that will serve them in a variety of professional settings. UMass Amherst recognizes several types of field experiences including: Internship Cooperative Education (Co-op) Practicum Research Experiences for Undergraduates (REU) Shadowing Service Learning/Community Service Independent Study Fellowship Graduate Externship For more thorough field experience definitions, visit the Field Experience Definition page . Defining Internships An internship is a position within an organization that provides students with a professional-level training experience and/or gives them the opportunity to explore a specific career. Internships happen year-round: during the fall or spring semester, over winter break, or during the summer. During the academic semester, students can go on internship full or part-time while also taking classes. Unlike a part-time job, however, an internship has a career focus and includes learning objectives. Students bring the knowledge they have learned in the classroom and apply this knowledge to the internship experience where they gain skills and further understanding of the industry. The workplace supervisor provides guidance and feedback to facilitate the learning process. The employer also provides an evaluation at the end of the internship. There are a range of experiences that qualify as “internship” including those for pay, those for credit, micro-internships, and formal internship programs. UMass Amherst adheres to and expects employers to follow Fair Labor Standards Act for unpaid internships. UMass Amherst faculty, not the employer, determine if an internship is eligible for credit. Note that credit must be both earned and paid for by the student. To be eligible for credit, students must work for an employer for a minimum of 40 hours and complete academic work (a project, a writing assignment, an art/music piece, a computer model, for example) as assigned by their academic sponsor. Employer Responsibilities for Internships & Co-ops Develop a Position Description: Detailed expectations will make both the intern's and the employer's lives easier. You will be treating the intern as a professional, being especially careful to provide opportunities for training and mentoring, as per federal labor law. Provide an On-site Supervisor for the student and include project-related substantial work in a job description. Prepare your Staff: Make sure your staff understands the intern's role and how the intern will fit into the office and organization. Have people suggest projects and help orient the intern. Introduce the Intern: A good orientation will include a tour of the site and introductions to the intern's co-workers. Clarify your expectations, their tasks, and the supervisory structure. Provide Training and Challenges: Offer diverse and challenging duties. Include the intern in regular meetings and appropriate events, and provide opportunities for intern to connect with a mentor. Train the intern in the appropriate working skills. Understand that they want to prove their capabilities - integrate them as useful and contributing members of work groups as soon as possible. Their confidence and level of productivity will quickly increase. Give Feedback: Schedule regular supervisory meetings during the intern’s stay. You will be asked to fill out a final evaluation for each student at the end of the placement period. What are the Essential Components of an Internship? Internships need to be meaningful and related to the student’s major or provide exploration into a career path of interest to the student. An intentional focus on student learning and application of knowledge gained in the classroom Clearly defined learning objectives/goals related to the student’s major Work duties often centered around a defined project Supervision by a professional who can provide training, direction, and routine feedback What does UMass need for the relationship to be successful? Flexible work schedules for students on internship during the academic semester (they have classes!) Adhere to our guidelines for hiring and managing students Post internship opportunities on Handshake. For a guide to using Handshake, visit Post Jobs and Internships . Internship Project Ideas Project Ideas We would encourage employers to think about projects they may be able to assign to a student that would be beneficial for both the student and the organization. Many projects we see students perform center around the following areas: Communications & Marketing Data, Analytics, Statistics Research Technology, Security, Website, Databases Creative Arts, Design Finance & Accounting Writing, Editing, Content management Sales, Lead creation, Database clean-up, Metrics evaluation Lab & Environmental data collection and/or analysis Internship Compensation Employers are strongly encouraged to pay students fair market value for their work or at least minimum wage, regardless of whether students choose to apply to earn academic credit for the experience. Advantages of paid internships: Broaden the applicant pool: Participating in an unpaid internship is much harder, and often impossible, for students who cannot forgo a paycheck for a significant period. Encourage a diverse workforce: If diversity is something your organization values, removing financial barriers for internships will better align with these values. Unpaid internships must meet the requirements set by the U.S. Department of Labor (DOL) Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) . In June 2023, the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) released the Position Statement on Unpaid Internships and the Need for Federal Action , a research and data supported article which discusses the following: Internships provide the critical link to launching careers (data supported) Unpaid internships are problematic (data supported) Unpaid internships are a barrier to achieving equity and opportunity for all college students Call to policymakers for action Internship Subsidy Programs for Massachusetts Employers Several Massachusetts agencies have developed internship subsidy and reimbursement programs to promote growth within their respective fields. We encourage you to explore these opportunities. Massachusetts Life Science Center - Investing in the next generation of life science professionals is at the core of our mission. The Internship Challenge enhances the talent pipeline for the Massachusetts life sciences industry by creating hundreds of new internship opportunities each year for college students and recent graduates interested in life sciences careers. The workforce development program connects employers with prospective interns through an online platform and enables eligible companies to pay their interns for up to twelve weeks by subsidizing intern wages. Massachusetts Clean Energy Center - Take advantage of MassCEC’s award-winning Clean Energy Internship Program to find the talent you need at a rate you can afford. MassCEC reimburses host employers who hire clean energy interns through our program, so you can take on additional help for little to no cost. Non-Profit Organizations - If you are an employer located in Massachusetts as a nonprofit community service organization with an active 501(c)3, you may be eligible to hire students using Federal Work-Study funds. See the UMass Community Service link for more information and guidelines. Micro-Internships UMass has partnered with Parker Dewey to offer an innovative opportunity for our corporate and nonprofit partners to offer short-term, professional, paid work experiences to current students through Micro-Internships. Micro-Internships allow students to demonstrate their skills, explore career paths, and develop their professional networks Unlike a traditional internship, these paid opportunities typically range from 10 to 40 hours of work, and most can be completed remotely Micro-Internships can provide you with short-term, project-based help from current students and recent grads, making it easier than ever to get support on professional tasks that may not be the best use of your team's time Not only does this help the students, but it allows you as employers to get immediate support and enhance your company’s pipeline for future interns and new hires To learn more or start posting assignments, visit Parker Dewey . Tips for Writing a Great Student Internship Posting A well thought out internship posting will make students excited about what your company does and the opportunity to learn within your organization. And most importantly, it will proactively answer most of the questions a student may have about the internship, which will make it more likely they will hit the apply button. These are the important sections for writing a great Student Internship Posting: Internship Job Title Use a job title that has meaning to a college student and will also look attractive on their resume The job title should include the description of the job function and the word “intern” or “internship” Also include the season/year you are seeking to have an intern work (ex: Fall 2022 or Summer 2023) Company Description Briefly explain what your company does and/or the mission Highlight what makes your organization unique; why would someone want to work here? Position Description Describe the role the intern will perform Explain how this internship experience will benefit the company Describe the learning benefits and outcomes for the student Desired Qualifications Professional skills (often referred to as soft skills) Technical skills and experience School year and/or expected graduation date Relevant Details – this may include the following: Compensation Length of internship opportunity Desired schedule and/or hours Virtual, In-Person, or Hybrid work schedule Application instructions and deadlines Work attire Include accessibility by public transportation if relevant Virtual Internship Best Practices Based upon National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) guidelines and conversations with both students and employers, UMass Amherst has put together the following list of best practices for virtual internships. Cover the Basics Since virtual work can have a certain amount of “disconnect” built in, it is important to ensure the work is meaningful. This can be alleviated through providing interns work that gives them a chance to connect with and contribute to the goals of the organization. Find ways to immerse interns into the company culture. Build community by including interaction in the schedule with other interns and employees. Manager Training Ensure managers are ready to manage interns on a remote basis by providing them with tools to aid in success working in a virtual world. Mitigate Challenges It is best to not assume students have access to the technology and internet required to perform their job. You may want to have a conversation about this in advance to provide them the tools needed to get their job done. Interns working from home may not have a private space available to them for work, so please don’t misinterpret a lack of privacy as a lack of professionalism. Keep to a Schedule As much as possible, set and adhere to a schedule over the course of the internship. The schedule does not need to be static, but students work best when they know what is expected and when. Keep Lines of Communication Open Schedule regular check-ins with interns and provide ongoing feedback over the course of the internships experience. NACE 15 Best Practices for Internship Programs NACE Career Readiness Competencies – For new college graduates, career readiness is key to ensuring successful entrance into the workforce. Career readiness is the foundation upon which a successful career is launched. For employers, career readiness offers a framework for developing talent through internship and other experiential learning opportunities. Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
    },
    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/employers/recruiting-guidelines": {
      "title": "Recruiting Guidelines",
      "description": "Recruiting Guidelines : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Recruiting Guidelines Careers Employers Recruiting Guidelines Close Menu Employers: Quick Links Handshake Login Career Fairs UMass Amherst Academic Calendar Contact Us All recruitment through the University of Massachusetts Amherst (UMass Amherst) career centers must adhere to Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO) guidelines , the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) Principles for Ethical Professional Practice , and the career center’s terms and conditions detailed below, which are subject to change without prior notice. Career centers reserve the rights to suspend, terminate or modify access and/or recruitment privileges for any reason, in their sole discretion, including failure to abide by these policies and guidelines. Non-Discrimination Policy UMass Amherst is committed in policy, principle, and practice to maintaining an environment which prohibits discriminatory behavior and provides equal opportunity for all persons. UMass Amherst prohibits discrimination on the basis of race (including traits historically associated with race, including, but not limited to, hair texture, hair type, hair length and protective hairstyles), color, religion, creed, sex, age, marital status, national origin, mental or physical disability, political belief or affiliation, veteran status, sexual orientation, gender identity and expression, genetic information, citizenship/immigration status and any other class of individuals protected from discrimination under state or federal law in any aspect of the access to, admission, or treatment of students in its programs and activities, or in the recruitment, employment and application for employment. Hiring International Students The University of Massachusetts Amherst attracts competitive talent from over 100 countries. We encourage our employer partners to consider hiring international students for your internships, co-ops, and full-time positions. Bringing their unique benefits to your organization is easier than you might think. Partners report that the international students they hire: Offer cross-cultural competencies and proficiency in multiple languages Provide high level skills such as leadership, independence, and adaptability Bring the benefits of prior education and employment experience abroad Imagine and implement innovative approaches to doing business in the global economy Add diversity to an organization's competitive workforce Citizenship/Immigration Status and National Origin Discrimination The Immigration and Nationality Act prohibits citizenship/immigration status and national origin discrimination with respect to hiring, termination, and recruiting or referring for a fee. Citizenship/immigration status discrimination includes requiring U.S. citizenship for employment (unless a narrow exception applies) or only recruiting workers on OPT/STEM OPT or on an H-1B visa. National origin discrimination includes treating individuals differently because of their place of birth, country of origin, ancestry, first language, accent or because they are perceived as looking or sounding \"foreign.” Employers should follow the best practices set forth by the Department of Justice . Unless legally required, job postings cannot limit eligibility based on citizenship or immigration status, such as \"Only U.S. Citizens\" or “H-1Bs and OPT Preferred.\" Job postings cannot limit eligibility based on national origin, such as “Native English Speakers only.” Employers are not obliged to sponsor a candidate for work authorization, i.e., immigration sponsorship. An employer is allowed to disqualify a candidate because they lack work authorization. Employers may use the following language when they do not want to engage in immigration sponsorship: “Applicants must be authorized to work in the US.” Employers may not ask candidates specific questions about their work authorization or ask them to produce documentation until an offer is made and accepted. Employers may ask the following questions regarding an applicant’s U.S. work authorization: Are they currently work authorized? Will they now or in the future require work authorization sponsorship from their employer? Don’t use the ITAR or the EAR as a reason to limit jobs to candidates with certain citizenships, immigration statutes, or national origins – don’t limit jobs to U.S. citizens because the job involves assessing export-controlled items. Job Postings All job listings are posted at the discretion of the UMass Amherst Career Centers. We reserve the right to decline to post jobs that do not support the interests of UMass Amherst and our students. UMass Career Services does not accept or post positions that: Require a fee for training or participation. Would employ students at a private residence. Would employ students by a private individual with an unestablished business and no employment or work-for-hire contract. UMass Amherst requires that all employers recruiting UMass Amherst students adhere to Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO) guidelines, the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) Principles for Ethical Professional Practice, and the terms and conditions set forth by UMass Amherst’s career centers. As such, any job postings shared by employers on Handshake and that are accessible by UMass Amherst students are expected to be in-line within these defined guidelines and compliant with federal and state law. Students are encouraged to apply to any postings of interest, regardless of any limitations on immigration/citizenship status stated in the job description. For information on positions that may exempt applications based on immigration/citizenship status, visit the Immigrant and Employee Rights Section website. UMass Amherst students are expected to do their due diligence while reviewing job applications by considering the qualifications for each job carefully before applying. Neither UMass Amherst nor Handshake will prevent you from applying for any position listed on Handshake.com. Compliance Message Regarding Unpaid Internships Employers are strongly encouraged to pay students fair market value for their work or at least minimum wage, regardless of whether students choose to apply to earn academic credit for the experience. Advantages of paid internships: Broaden the applicant pool: Participating in an unpaid internship is much harder, and often impossible, for students who cannot forgo a paycheck for a significant period. Encourage a diverse workforce: If diversity is something your organization values, removing financial barriers for internships will better align with these values. For-Profit Employers: Unpaid internships must meet the requirements set by the U.S. Department of Labor (DOL) Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) . Non-Profit Organizations: If you are an employer located in Massachusetts as a nonprofit community service organization with an active 501(c)(3), you may be eligible to hire students using Federal Work-Study funds. See the UMass Community Service link for more information and guidelines. In June 2023, the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) released the Position Statement on Unpaid Internships and the Need for Federal Action , a research and data supported article which discusses the following: Internships provide the critical link to launching careers (data supported) Unpaid internships are problematic (data supported) Unpaid internships are a barrier to achieving equity and opportunity for all college students Call to policymakers for action Employment Offer Guidelines We are pleased that many companies are interested in hiring our students from the University of Massachusetts Amherst. As a member of NACE (National Association of Colleges and Employers), we adhere to their professional recruiting principles and believe that it is in the best interests of students and employers if students are given a reasonable amount of time to consider alternative offers before making important career decisions. While NACE does not address specific time frames, UMass Amherst is aware that employer hiring patterns have shifted more competitively within recent years in recruitment processes and work with shorter response windows for offers. Understanding this, we provide these fair guidelines to both students and employers. If students are offered: Full-time positions or internships during the fall or spring semester : students will have at least 3 weeks from the receipt of the written offer to accept/decline offers. Full-time positions or second internships (with the same company) after summer internships : students will have until November 1 to accept/decline offers. This allows students time to participate in fall on-campus recruiting if they so choose. We discourage employers from using practices that would improperly induce early acceptances any time of the year. Specifically, we expect that any employer that collaborates with UMass Career Services to abide by these offer guidelines. Third Party Recruiters The National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) defines \"third-party recruiters\" as: agencies, organizations, or individuals recruiting candidates for temporary, part-time, or full-time employment opportunities other than for their own needs. This includes entities that refer or recruit for profit or not for profit, and it includes agencies that collect student information to be disclosed to employers for purposes of recruitment and employment. UMass Amherst identifies the following types of organizations as “third-party recruiters”: employment agencies, staffing services, temporary agencies, on line job boards, recruiters, and contractors. Representatives from these companies may post under the following conditions: They must fully disclose the name of the client that the third-party recruiter is representing and to whom the students’ credentials will be disclosed. They must keep all student information obtained confidential unless receiving additional written student consent. This standard complies with Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA). They must not retain any student resumes for future placement unless given additional student written consent. They must not charge a fee to the applicant. Multilevel Marketing / Student Ambassador Positions UMass Amherst Career Services does not endorse companies or any student ambassador positions in which our students are asked to solicit other students, students’ families or faculty for a company’s services/products or are asked to pay out their own money to get started. Many career centers across the country adhere to this policy. Cannabis Company Recruitment Policy UMass Career Services does not allow on-campus recruitment and will not approve job or internship postings for organizations involved with the use, production, testing, or distribution of recreational or medical marijuana or any by-products that exceed the legal THC level.  Because UMass receives federal funds, we follow federal laws regarding THC-containing products. Policy on Alcohol in College Recruiting The University of Massachusetts Amherst and its career centers are members of the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE), the professional association for college recruiters and career services professionals. As members, we abide by the NACE Principles for Professional Practice, a document that serves as the ethical framework and foundation for practices within the career services and recruiting fields. The Principles’ document states \"Serving alcohol should not be part of the recruitment process on or off campus. This includes receptions, dinners, company tours, etc.\"  UMass Amherst Career Services requests that you support the no-alcohol policy while recruiting our students. Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
    },
    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/employers/contact-us": {
      "title": "Contact Us",
      "description": "Contact Us : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Contact Us In addition to a centralized career center, we have college-based career centers to help you target your hiring. The goal of our structure is to provide major-specific career content knowledge to students while also providing efficient, \"one-stop\" service to employers who are looking to engage with students across disciplines. We also have a dedicated career services professional on our Charles River Campus in Newton, MA. If you are seeking specific major(s), please let a contact below know how you would like to connect with our students. Inquiries Across Multiple Colleges Tiffany Appleton | [email protected] Overall Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 24,019 Total Graduate Students: 7,299 Employers UMass Employer Relations Humanities & Fine Arts Lisa Blacher | [email protected] Humanities & Fine Arts Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 2,074 Total Graduate Students: 525 HFA Career Center HFA Career Center Education Dr. Elise Carrier | [email protected] Education Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 509 Total Graduate Students: 403 Contact Elise Contact Elise Social and Behavioral Sciences Carol Sharick | [email protected] Social and Behavior Sciences Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 4,569 Total Graduate Students: 656 SBS Career & Professional Development SBS Career & Professional Development Public Health and Health Sciences Aimee Gilbert Loinaz | [email protected] Public Health and Health Sciences Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 1,728 Total Graduate Students: 442 Public Health and Health Sciences Office of Career Planning Public Health and Health Sciences Office of Career Planning Nursing Dr. Elise Carrier | [email protected] Nursing Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 339 Total Graduate Students: 147 Contact Elise Contact Elise Isenberg School of Management Melissa Salva | [email protected] Isenberg School of Management Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 4,244 Total Graduate Students: 2,106 Chase Career Center Chase Career Center Natural Sciences Janet Bordwin | [email protected] Natural Sciences Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 8,255 Total Graduate Students: 1,048 CNS Career Center CNS Career Center Information and Computer Sciences Vera Palmer | [email protected] Information and Computer Sciences Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 1,720 Total Graduate Students: 892 CICS Careers CICS Careers University Without Walls Joanna Miles | [email protected] UWW Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 903 Career Services for Interdisciplinary Students Career Services for Interdisciplinary Students Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration Joanna Miles | [email protected] BDIC Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 244 BDIC Career Services BDIC Career Services Riccio College of Engineering Todd Butynski | [email protected] Engineering Enrollment Statistics (Fall 2025): Total Undergraduate Students: 2,723 Total Graduate Students: 646 Engineering Career Development and Experiential Learning Center Engineering Career Development and Experiential Learning Center Charles River Campus Rachel Evenson | [email protected] The Charles River Campus of UMass Amherst serves as a center for experiential learning, high-impact practices, industry/alumni engagement, and professional development in the greater Boston area. The campus is located at 100 Carlson Avenue in Newton, MA. Charles River Campus Charles River Campus Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
