    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/people/engineering-career-community": {
      "title": "Engineering Career Community",
      "description": "Engineering Career Community : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Join the Engineering Career Community Learn More Engineering Career Community Engineering careers are diverse fields that involve various aspects of design, development, and innovation. These roles are crucial in creating new products and improving existing processes to enhance efficiency, effectiveness, and sustainability. Whether designing cutting-edge technologies, optimizing manufacturing systems, or developing environmentally friendly solutions, engineers play a key role in shaping how industries operate. With a blend of math, science, and technical expertise, engineers continue to drive advancements by creating tangible solutions based on society’s needs. Is This Field Right for Me? Careers in Environment: Occupational Outlook Handbook Overall employment in architecture and engineering occupations is projected to grow 4 percent from 2021 to 2031, about as fast as the average for all occupations; this increase is expected to result in about 91,300 new jobs over the decade. In addition to new jobs from growth, opportunities arise from the need to replace workers who leave their occupations permanently. About 200,900 openings each year, on average, are projected to come from growth and replacement needs Careers in Environment: Occupational Outlook Handbook Read More How to Know If You Should Major in Engineering Engineering Expectations provides an excellent list of technical and soft skills that are important for engineers. How to Know If You Should Major in Engineering Read More Jobs In This Field Top Careers in Engineering An engineer can find suitable roles in the technology, construction, industrial or aeronautics industries. Understanding the diverse types of engineering careers can help you learn more about their nature of work and career advancement opportunities. In this article, we discuss the primary duties and national average salaries of top careers in engineering. Top Careers in Engineering Read More Career Profiles in Engineering Engineering is an exciting, dynamic, and ever evolving career field and there are growing opportunities for qualified engineers. This section provides a wealth of relevant and reliable information on various engineering careers. Whether you are looking for information on becoming a mechanical engineer or simply want to explore a day in the life of a general engineer, you will find what you are looking for here. Career Profiles in Engineering Read More Certifications and LinkedIn Learning Recommendations Introduction to Environmental Analytics Instructor and analytics engineer Amataverna Lee guides you through what analytics engineering is, why it matters, and which roles can transition most easily into analytics engineering. She explains data modeling, cloud data warehouses, data pipeline tools, and business intelligence tools. Introduction to Environmental Analytics Read More Reverse Engineering Foundations Join instructor Steven Marjieh as he acquaints you with the fundamentals of reverse engineering, which allows you to infer a model from an object and work with it in your own designs. Learn how to use physical measurement, 3D scanning, and touch probes to measure real-world objects and work with them in your CAD tool of choice. Reverse Engineering Foundations Read More Electronics Foundations Barron demonstrates how to build basic circuits using resistors, capacitors, and inductors. He dives into how each component works and shows the common circuits that use them. He explains the difference between alternating current and direct current and how to use an oscilloscope to view electrical signals. Then, he demonstrates building passive filter circuits to remove unwanted frequency components from those signals. Electronics Foundations Read More Explore a Career in Data Engineering Begin your career working with core data engineering tools and platforms. This foundational learning path will take you from fundamental data engineering concepts and skills to implementing a full data pipeline. Explore a Career in Data Engineering Read More AutoCAD Essentials Instructor Shaun Bryant covers the basics of the AutoCAD Mechanical 2022 user interface and leads you step-by-step through producing precise, measured mechanical engineering drawings and designs. Shaun teaches you how to structure drawings and data, create and edit geometry, generate parts from the library, and annotate drawings. AutoCAD Essentials Read More How to Apply Engineer Jobs Self-proclaimed “the world’s most-visited engineering job site,” EngineerJobs.com hosts one of the biggest search engines for engineering jobs around the world. This search engine currently advertises hundreds of thousands of engineering jobs from some of the largest engineering firms in the world. You can perform targeted searches for specific engineering jobs according to your specialty and career needs in various locations, industries, and sub-industries. Engineer Jobs Read More Engineering Central Engineering Central is a dedicated site to engineering jobs. At the time of this writing, you can apply to 205 Electrical Engineering Jobs, 164 Mechanical Engineering Jobs, 158 Chemical Engineering Jobs, and opportunities in Civil Engineering, Manufacturing Engineering, and Industrial Engineering. The majority of these jobs are for engineering companies in North America. However, you will find opportunities posted for other global locations as well. Engineering Central Read More Engineering.com Engineering.com is another dedicated job search engine that covers the engineering jobs sector. It offers the best engineering jobs for engineers across the United States and Canada. Searching engineering jobs by specialty, location, and targeted filters such as work experience and seniority level make finding engineering jobs simple on this platform. Check out this job search engine for opportunities in mechanical engineering jobs, electrical engineering jobs, biomedical engineering jobs, chemical engineering jobs, and more. Engineering.com Read More Communities at UMass and Beyond In addition to joining the Connect UMass Career Community for Engineering , there are many other ways you can meet and collaborate with people in these industries. UMass Registered Student Organizations of Interest M5 MIE Innovation Lab ChemE Car Concrete Canoe Engineers Without Borders (EWB) Formula SAE HackUMass MinuteMan Launch Team/Rocket Team UMass Robotics Seismic Design Team Steel Bridge Team Supermileage Vehicle Team Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) Team Professional Associations Black Women in Engineering and Computing (BWEC) National Society of Black Engineers (NSBE) Out in STEM (oSTEM) Society of American Military Engineers Society of Asian Scientists and Engineers (SASE) Society of Hispanic Professional Engineers (SHPE) Society of Women Engineers (SWE) Professional Orgs with UMASS Chapters Associated General Contractors (AGC) American Institute of Chemical Engineers (AIChE) American Society of Civil Engineers (ASCE) American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME) Biomedical Engineering Society (BMES) Chi Epsilon Eta Kappa Nu (HKN) Institute of Industrial and Systems Engineers (IISE) Institute of Electrical & Electronics Engineers (IEEE) International Society of Pharmaceutical Engineers (ISPE) Institute of Transportation Engineers (ITE) Pi Tau Sigman Tau Beta Pi (TBP) Contact Information Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
    },
    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/people/government-and-public-service-career-community": {
      "title": "Government and Public Service Career Community",
      "description": "Government and Public Service Career Community : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Join the Government and Public Service Career Community Learn More Government and Public Service Career Community Government and Public Service careers are fueled by the strong desire to help others and change the world. They are the movers and shakers of society, ensuring equal access to resources and safeguarding the welfare of communities. As society evolves, the demand for effective governance grows, presenting opportunities to drive change and solve complex social issues. Public service professionals play a critical role in shaping laws, regulations, and public initiatives, using a combination of leadership, strategic thinking, and public administration expertise to create a positive and lasting impact on society. Is This Field Right for Me? Learn about USAJOBS hiring paths for specific groups. The federal government offers unique hiring paths to help hire individuals that represent our diverse society. Learn more about each hiring path and your eligibility. Learn about USAJOBS hiring paths for specific groups. Read More 12 Must Have Skills for Public Service Careers If you have a passion for helping others and want to work out in the community, a career in public service and administration could be a good fit for you. These careers put you in the right place to provide true help to people who need it most. Yet passion alone is not enough to make your career a success. There are specific skills you need to succeed as a public servant. From leadership skills to the ability to navigate a complex political landscape, learn more about the skills you need to fill a key role in your local community. 12 Must Have Skills for Public Service Careers Read More Jobs in this Field 18 Types of Government Jobs The federal government employs many people in multiple job types across various markets. You may find jobs in administration, finances, security, clerical, construction and design within various departments and organizations 18 Types of Government Jobs Read More Pathways Program The Pathways Program offers federal internship and employment opportunities for current students, recent graduates, and those with an advanced degree. Pathways Program Read More Learn about USAJOBS hiring paths for specific groups. The federal government offers unique hiring paths to help hire individuals that represent our diverse society. Learn more about each hiring path and your eligibility. Learn about USAJOBS hiring paths for specific groups. Read More Certifications and LinkedIn Learning Recommendations Create a culture of service A culture of service extends far beyond your customer service department. Developing a service-oriented organization or team requires clarity of messaging and operating through a lens of impact. In this course, leadership consultants Lisa Earle McLeod and Elizabeth McLeod help leaders communicate their customer-focused purpose and outline processes and policies that support these values. Create a culture of service Read More Developing Growth Mindset As a leader, developing a growth mindset is key to your success. The way you overcome challenges, build relationships, and cultivate a positive culture all stems from your mental agility. In this course, instructor Karen Allen shows you how to strengthen your greatest asset: your mindset. Developing Growth Mindset Read More Leading in Government In this course, Adelle Dantzler helps to equip you with the critical knowledge and skills needed to excel in a government leadership role. After detailing what makes leading in government different from leading anywhere else, Adelle shares practical tips for using strategic thinking to handle common public leadership situations. Leading in Government Read More Project Management: Government Projects Learn the nuances of managing government projects—a subset of project management with tougher deadlines, tighter budgets, and stricter requirements. Certified PMP® Oliver Yarbrough reviews the government project lifecycle from obtaining a government contract to executing the project and closing it out. Project Management: Government Projects Read More How to Apply Unlike other hiring fields, Government agencies must be transparent with all their postings. You will find them all at: www.usajobs.gov Associated nonprofit and policy institutes can be found at: www.idealist.org Recent Graduate Hiring Programs: https://www.opm.gov/policy-data-oversight/hiring-information/students-recent-graduates/ ***When applying, search for listings at a GS-5 or GS-7 for undergraduate students, a GS 9 and above for those graduating with an advanced degree. Many entry level jobs for students will have a designation in their listings, additionally. Communities at UMass and Beyond In addition to joining the Connect UMass Career Community for Government and Public Service , there are many other ways you can meet and collaborate with people in these industries. UMass Registered Student Organizations of Interest Student Government Assocation Youth in Government (YMCA) Center for Educaton and Policy University Democrats Republican Club Policy and Administrative Grad Council Professional Associations Partnership for Public Service American Public Works Assocation Federal Assocation of Managers Foreign Policy Assocation Society of Government Meeting Officials Professional Services Council Contact Information Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
    },
    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/connections/career-communities/healthcare-life-sciences-and-nursing-career-community": {
      "title": "Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Career Community",
      "description": "Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Career Community : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Join the Healthcare and Life Sciences Career Community Learn More Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Career Community Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing careers are defined by their study with living organisms and life processes. This field encompasses the research and practice of medicine, nutrition, and other health-related issues, exploring their effects on both humans and animals. As medical knowledge advances, so does our understanding of health, wellness, and disease prevention, creating new opportunities for improving quality of life. Professionals in these fields play a vital role in discovering treatments, improving prevention, and enhancing our understanding of biological systems for all living beings. Is This Field Right for Me? Health Sciences Career Overview A health science major studies health and disease in the human body. Students in this major are prepared to work in a variety of health-related careers and settings and to pursue graduate-level education. Health science majors typically value quality of health care, health promotion and disease prevention, so many dedicate their careers to improving the lives of their patients and the community. Health Sciences Career Overview Read More 11 Rewarding Careers in Science For those with a passion for scientific research and the natural world, a career in life science can be an excellent option. Life sciences apply to a wide range of industries, from environmental and animal science to human health and medicine. Learning about the life sciences and some careers in this field can help you plan your career path and decide what job is right for you. In this article, we define what life science is and offer 11 career options in the life sciences with job descriptions and salary information to help you plan your career. 11 Rewarding Careers in Science Read More Jobs in this Field The most in Demand Health Science Jobs The COVID-19 pandemic has changed every aspect of life for most Americans, including their careers. One field seeing exponential job growth is health science. According to the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS), six of the 10 fastest-growing occupations are related to healthcare. Many of these occupations do not involve direct patient care. The most in Demand Health Science Jobs Read More Industry Profile from Bureau of Labor Statics for Life Science Careers The Occupational Outlook Handbook (OOH) is an online career guidance resource that provides information on hundreds of occupations in the United States. Industry Profile from Bureau of Labor Statics for Life Science Careers Read More Certifications and LinkedIn Learning Recommendations The Data Science of Healthcare, Medicine, and Public Health This nontechnical course explores a variety of ways to apply data science to medicine and public health. Data scientist Barton Poulson discusses the ways that dramatically increased quantities of data—from genetic testing, genealogy, brain scans, and other sources—are changing the theory and practice of healthcare. The Data Science of Healthcare, Medicine, and Public Health Read More Using Public Health Dashboards Learn how to evaluate the various dashboards that are available to researchers, including the COVID-19 dashboard from John Hopkins University and the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation (IHME) dashboard, in this course with data scientist and professor Jennifer Shin. Learn how to view and interpret dashboard metrics and visualizations and use them effectively in your own data science projects. Using Public Health Dashboards Read More 5 Learning Lessons from Cognitive Science In this course from Knowable, Peter C. Brown, author of Make It Stick: The Science of Successful Learning, draws on the latest research from the fields of cognitive science and psychology to make you a more powerful, productive learner. Explore five science-backed techniques to learn faster and retain more (active retrieval, spaced retrieval, interleaving, reflection, and trials), learn to break the bad mental habits you might have picked up in school, and prime your mind to reach its full potential. 5 Learning Lessons from Cognitive Science Read More Medical Imaging with Azure The AI (Artificial Intelligence) Show is a licensed partnership with Microsoft Learn that provides a platform to showcase the progress taking place in the field of AI at Microsoft. In this course, Seth Juarez, Principal Program Manager in the Azure Artificial Intelligence Product Group, is accompanied by Harmke Alkemade and Andreas Kopp. Harmke and Andreas discuss their development of a GitHub repository containing examples of leveraging Azure Machine Learning for Medical Imaging use-cases. The course includes a detailed overview of the repository and practical demonstrations on how data scientists and medical professionals can leverage the content. Medical Imaging with Azure Read More How to Apply REU (Research Experiences for Undergraduates) NSF funds many research opportunities for undergraduate students through its REU Sites program. An REU Site consists of ten or so undergraduates who work in the host institution's research programs. Each student is associated with a specific research project, where he/she works closely with the faculty and other researchers REU (Research Experiences for Undergraduates) Read More Mass Life Sciences Center Internship Challenge The Internship Challenge enhances the talent pipeline for the Massachusetts life sciences industry by creating hundreds of new internship opportunities each year for college students and recent graduates interested in life sciences careers. Mass Life Sciences Center Internship Challenge Read More Mass Bio Career Center MassBio’s mission is to advance Massachusetts’ leadership in the life sciences to grow the industry, add value to the healthcare system, and improve patient lives. Mass Bio Career Center Read More BioSpace BioSpace is the digital hub for life science news and jobs. BioSpace Read More Careers at CDC (Centers for Disease Control) CDC scientists work 24/7 to protect the world from public health threats. Careers at CDC (Centers for Disease Control) Read More CDC: Public Health Associate Program The Public Health Associate Program (PHAP) is a competitive, two-year, paid training program with the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). Associates are assigned to state, tribal, local, and territorial public health agencies and nongovernmental organizations and work with other professionals across various public health settings. CDC: Public Health Associate Program Read More NIH: Student Careers Join the world’s largest biomedical research agency in one of NIH’s many programs for students and recent graduates. Our internship and entry-level positions provide unique Pathways into scientific and administrative careers. NIH: Student Careers Read More The American Nurses Association The American Nurses Association (ANA) is the premier organization representing the interests of the nation's 3.6 million registered nurses. ANA advances the nursing profession by fostering high standards of nursing practice, promoting a safe and ethical work environment, bolstering the health and wellness of nurses, and advocating on health care issues that affect nurses and the public. The American Nurses Association Read More iHireTherapy iHireTherapy is a niche job board dedicated solely to the Therapy industry; they bring Therapy jobs and Therapy job seekers together to make successful employment possible. iHireTherapy Read More Communities at UMass and Beyond In addition to joining the Connect UMass Career Community for Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing , there are many other ways you can meet and collaborate with people in these industries. UMass Registered Student Organizations of Interest Life Science Café Pre-Vet and Animal Science Minority Association of Premed Food Science Club Science Outreach Club Association of Women in Science Polymer Science Club Life Science Research Council Chemistry Club Geoscience Club Omega Psi Phi Kinesiology Club Public Health Club Genetics Club Pre-Med Society Campus Emergency Response American Medical Student Association Global Medicine Brigade Black Women in Medicine Future Health Professionals Professional Associations American Public Health Association American Nursing Association National Medical Association American Dental Association Annual Biomedical Research Conference for Minoritized Scientists (ABRCMS) Scientific Societies American Association for the Advancement of Science American Institute of Biological Sciences National Academies of Science American Fisheries Society American Ornithological Society American Society of Mammalogists American Society of Naturalists Animal Behavior Society Botanical Society of America Ecological Society of America Entomological Society of America Society for Conservation Biology American Physiological Society American Society for Biochemistry and Molecular Biology American Society for Cell Biology American Society of Human Genetics American Society for Microbiology American Society of Plant Biologists Biophysical Society Federation of American Societies for Experimental Biology Society for Developmental Biology Contact Information Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
    },
    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/people/infrastructure-and-transportation-career-community": {
      "title": "Infrastructure and Transportation Career Community",
      "description": "Infrastructure and Transportation Career Community : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Join the Infrastructure and Transportation Career Community Learn More Infrastructure and Transportation Career Community Infrastructure and Transportation careers shape the world we live in and keep it running smoothly. These roles are focused on designing, building, and maintaining the systems that keep our society connected and functioning. From transportation networks that move goods and people to utilities that power our homes and businesses, these systems are crucial for our daily lives. With a blend of technical expertise and creative problem-solving, professionals in these fields work to build and sustain the infrastructure that supports both today’s needs and tomorrow’s growth. Is this Field Right for Me? Infrastructure plays an essential role in our day-to-day life because it includes facilities and structures such as roads and highways, bridges, airports, phone lines, water and sewage treatment facilities, and power generating facilities. Careers in this field are impactful and jobs are universally available. Jobs in this Field From the Bureau of Labor Statistics : \"Most infrastructure-related occupations that require workers to have a bachelor’s degree are involved in planning and designing infrastructure projects. These include civil engineers and environmental engineers, along with surveyors, landscape architects, and architects, except landscape and naval, who also typically need to complete a paid internship, because an internship is generally required to obtain licensure. Two occupations that work to monitor and inspect infrastructure construction also typically require a bachelor’s degree for entry—construction  managers and health and safety engineers.” Certifications and LinkedIn Learning Recommendations 11 Infrastructure Certifications In this article, we discuss the importance of having an IT infrastructure certification and introduce 11 certifications you may wish to consider, with a comprehensive description of each. 11 Infrastructure Certifications Read More SAP Transportation Management Overview Learn the basics of transportation management (TM) from a business perspective—while you explore the SAP Transportation Management module and its processes. This course provides a solid foundation of the TM architecture, the usable master data in SAP, and related processes, from order capture to freight settlement. SAP Transportation Management Overview Read More Solve Supply Chain Transporation Problems with Excel Follow along with instructor Curt Frye as he shows how to use the Excel Solver add-in to minimize transportation and transshipment costs within a supply chain. Curt covers a range of topics, including how to analyze fixed costs and facility opening decisions, enforce level of service constraints, and perform scenario analysis. He also provides problem scenarios for you to solve, then goes through the solutions for each. Solve Supply Chain Transporation Problems with Excel Read More International Logistics In this course, Steven Brown covers the information that anyone who ships materials internationally needs to know. Steven covers topics like how logistics evolved globally, the special considerations unique to international logistics (including security and managing cultural differences), the different transportation modes available, and effective methods for controlling inventory. He also looks at the 11 international commercial terms, or “Incoterms,” that define the responsibilities of sellers and buyers in global trade. International Logistics Read More Understanding Logistics In this course, instructor Steven Brown shows you why logistics is important, what it is, and how logistics supports your company and your customers. Steven discusses how logistics must be effective and efficient. He explains steps to improve how a customer’s order is filled. The course covers both outbound and inbound logistics and goes over reverse logistics. More money is spent on transportation than on all other logistics functions combined, so Steven specifically takes you through different modes of transportation and how they often work together. Understanding Logistics Read More Supply Chain Foundations What is supply chain management (SCM)? It is the system that connects and integrates the links of sourcing, procurement, conversion, and logistics management. In this course, Professor Eddie Davila provides a detailed overview of all the links in the typical supply chain. Supply Chain Foundations Read More How to Apply In addition to Handshake, job boards for engineers and architects include: Engineering Jobs https://www.engineeringjobs.net/ National Society for Professional Engineers: https://www.nspe.org/resources/career-center Boston Society for Architecture: https://www.architects.org/jobs Archinect: https://archinect.com/jobs Communities at UMass and Beyond In addition to joining the Connect UMass Career Community for Infrastructure and Transportation , there are many other ways you can meet and collaborate with people in these industries. UMass Registered Student Organizations of Interest American Institute Architecture Students American Student Society of Civil Engineers Institute of Engineering Graduate Students Women's Transportation Seminar WTS (Women in Transportation Seminar) UMASS Chapter Professional Associations American Society of Civil Engineers New England Institute of Transportation Engineers Associated General Contractors Chi Epsilon Honor Society Contact Information Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
    },
    "https://www.umass.edu/careers/people/law-and-legal-services-career-community": {
      "title": "Law and Legal Services Career Community",
      "description": "Law and Legal Services Career Community : Career Development & Professional Connections : UMass Amherst Skip to main content The University of Massachusetts Amherst Open UMass Global Links Menu Visit Apply Give Search UMass.edu Menu Career Development & Professional Connections About Us Our Staff Career Centers Across UMass Our Newton Campus Handshake Login Student Outcomes Students Undergraduates Graduates Alumni Identity-Based Resources International Students Prospective Students UWW Students Employers Post Jobs and Internships Host an Event Career Fairs at UMass Why Recruit at UMass Internship Program Best Practices Recruiting Guidelines Contact Us Faculty Sponsor an Intern Career Events for Students Request a Presentation Resource Library Career Journey Expand Menu Item Self Discovery and Career Exploration Build Career Skills: Resumes, Job-searching, and Interviewing Learn Additional Technical, Creative, and Professional Skills Your Career Support Team Gain Experience: Internships, Co-ops, Research, and More! Is Graduate School Right for Me? Year-by-Year Checklist Tool Kits Expand Menu Item Resumes Cover Letters Career Fair Preparation Interviewing LinkedIn Learning Networking What Skills Do Employers Want? Applying to Grad School Photo Booth for Headshots Professional Wardrobe Closet Connections Expand Menu Item Career Communities Expand Menu Item Arts, Multimedia, and Entertainment Business, Financial Services, and Consulting Data and Information Technology Education, Human Services, and Counseling Engineering Government and Public Service Healthcare, Life Sciences, and Nursing Infrastructure and Transportation Law and Legal Services Research Sustainability, Environment, and Agriculture Career Centers Across UMass Expand Menu Item Bachelor's Degree with Individual Concentration (BDIC) Education Engineering Graduate School Office of Professional Development Humanities & Fine Arts (HFA) Information & Computer Sciences (CICS) Isenberg School of Management (ISOM) Natural Sciences (CNS) Nursing Public Health and Health Sciences (SPHHS) Social & Behavioral Sciences (SBS) University Without Walls (UWW) Connect with UMass Alumni Using LinkedIn How to Network Jobs & Internships Expand Menu Item Internships, Co-ops, and Other Field Experiences Expand Menu Item Field Experience Definitions Internships Co-ops Research Civic Engagement and Service Learning Internships and Co-Ops at Charles River Campus UMass Internship Partner Programs Scholarships for Field Experiences Jobs Searching for Jobs & Internships Can I Do an Internship? Negotiating Job Offers Interviewing Using Handshake Join the Law and Legal Services Career Community Learn More Law and Legal Services Career Community Law and Legal Service careers deal with constitutionality both in and out of court.These roles span a wide range of legal fields, from private to public and from business to criminal, addressing legal challenges in everyday life. Professionals in this field are key to managing and resolving legal issues wherever they arise. With a combination of legal expertise and problem-solving skills, they ensure that laws and regulations are followed, helping to maintain order and structure in various industries and sectors. Is This Field Right for Me? Careers in Law Careers in law run the gamut from paralegal to Doctor of Juridical Science. This article will introduce you to a variety of career pathways in the legal field, including several types of law degrees. Careers in Law Read More Should you go directly to Law School? The right time for law school will vary from person to person, but here is the good news: every year, first-year law students come from a mix of folks fresh out of undergraduate and those who have been working for a few years. Should you go directly to Law School? Read More Jobs in this Field 29 Areas of Law to Consider Areas of law can focus on specialties ranging from civil rights and environmental regulations to criminal or commercial litigation. Learning about the various fields you can pursue can help you choose a specialization that suits your interests and unique skill set. 29 Areas of Law to Consider Read More 14 Jobs in Law to Explore There are many jobs in law that you can pursue. Some positions involve studying the law while others focus on applying it to practical situations. Here are a few types of jobs you can consider if you are interested in a job where working with the law is a regular part of your duties. 14 Jobs in Law to Explore Read More Certifications and LinkedIn Learning Recommendations Think Like Lawyers to Make Decisions In this course, law professor Kim Wehle explains how thinking like a lawyer and employing the legal method of decision-making helps eliminate emotional reactivity, confirmation bias, and other decision-making pitfalls in favor of evidentiary-based analysis. Kim highlights key aspects of decision making and problem solving, like identifying stakeholders affected in any decision, the importance of asking the right questions, identifying, and prioritizing your goals, and exercising critical listening skills. Think Like Lawyers to Make Decisions Read More Ethics and Law This course—part of the Microsoft Professional Program offerings—explores the ethical and legal frameworks applicable to the data profession. Learn how these frameworks apply to practical problems posed by work in big data and data science and investigate applied data methods for ethical and legal work in analytics and AI. Ethics and Law Read More Negotiation Skills With a little practice, anyone can learn the simple rules of negotiation to get a great deal on a new car or house or achieve greater success at work by getting better deals with suppliers, customers, and even colleagues. Negotiation Skills Read More How to Apply Find Law FindLaw is a comprehensive site for legal professionals. Also, its Law Career Center provides many job listings for attorneys and others in the industry. Find Law Read More Law Crossing Law Crossing's job listings include more than 70,000 open legal positions from around the globe. The site lists jobs from about a quarter of a million legal employers, including law firms, governments, corporations, and more. Law Crossing Read More LawJobs.com This site bills itself as the premiere resource for legal jobs. In fact, Lawjobs.com provides users access to information about open positions for attorneys, legal aids, support staff, and others in the legal profession. LawJobs.com Read More Legal Staff Legal Staff is a great free option for locating key open positions in the legal field. The site’s search features are easy to use, and its dedicated focus on the legal industry ensures that you only see jobs that are relevant for your career. Legal Staff Read More Communities at UMass and Beyond In addition to joining the Connect UMass Career Community for L aw and Legal Services , there are many other ways you can meet and collaborate with people in these industries. UMass Registered Student Organizations of Interest Pre-law club Isenberg law club Student Legal Services Office Mass SPIRG Debate Roosevelt Institute Professional Associations American Bar Association (ABA) American Health Lawyers Association (AHLA) American Immigration Lawyers Association (AILA) Association of Corporate Counsel (ACC) Association of Eminent Domain Professionals (ADEP) Association of Legal Administrators (ALA) Association of Prosecuting Attorneys (APA) National Association for Legal Professionals (NALS) National Association of Administrative Law Judiciary (NAALJ) National Association of Consumer Advocates (NACA) National Association of Criminal Defense Lawyers (NACDL) National Association of Hearing Officials (NAHO) National Association of Legal Assistants (NALA) National Association of Legal Investigators (NALI) National Association of Legal Search Consultants (NALSC) National Association of Patent Practitioners (NAPP) National Association of Public Pension Attorneys (NAPPA) National Court Reporters Association (NCRA) National Employment Lawyers Association (NELA) National Notary Association (NNA) Contact Information Career Development & Professional Connections Whether you’re wondering where to begin, looking for an internship, or starting your job search, we will guide you through the process of your unique career development. Address: Goodell Hall | 140 Hicks Way | Amherst, MA 01003 Main Office Number: (413) 545-2224 Facebook Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Footer Students Career Journey Career Centers Resource Library Employers Handshake Contact Us UMass collegiate M - University of Massachusetts Amherst ©2025 University of Massachusetts Amherst · Site Policies · Accessibility"
